{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc","id":1485050,"title":"How do you manage your npc?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-06-26T13:53:00-07:00","id":1485050,"title":"How do you manage your npc?","content":"Do you have any advice on how to manage enemy ability?<br /> I use some exemple character sheet for few type of character but with all the powers available i can't fix something<br /> When i oppose npc to my friends i know their attributes and ability but i never know what powers give them. Vampires are the best exemple, their generation, their age and their clan there's too many variable to decide what kind of sheets i can create. I need 3 or 5 sheets but with all combination possible it's difficult to give them a set of powers","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-26T18:02:52-07:00","id":1485067,"title":"","content":"Don't make sheets for them. Assign them a pool for tasks they're good at (I.e. Physical 6, Mental 4, Social 3) and give them any power that seems suitable to them as a character. The fine nuances and mechanics are irrelevant. They are there to serve a narrative purpose, not to force you to have a million extra sheets to bean count.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-27T07:50:29-07:00","id":1485111,"title":"","content":"Just physical, mental ans social? Well that's way simpler but at the same times imprecise, unless you mean assigning a pool in the moment and remembering it (or writing it) for the rest of the scene.<br /> And what about their health level if you just assign a pool?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-27T09:20:34-07:00","id":1485115,"title":"","content":"Does it actually matter? It's your imagination and your world. Who knows better than you do what your NPC's are good at and what they are meant to do in your story.<br /> It doesn't matter if you decide on the fly their dicepool is 6,7 or 2.<br /> <br /> What about their health levels? Health levels are not affected by stats in the World Of Darkness. Most things in the World of Darkness have only 7 Health Levels, unless they're specialised monsters like in Werewolf or if you use some kind of rule for Extras. If there is actually combat I tend to just have a scrap piece of paper where I mark wounds for different characters, but it doesn't matter much unless they're important characters.<br /> <br /> Go easy on yourself and simplify things on the backend. There is more than enough work as a Storyteller to actually worry about stats meant to generate PC's and shape their advancement.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T00:14:59-07:00","id":1485176,"title":"","content":"Which edition are you using, BTW? I find it does make a difference.<br /> <br /> In V20, I just list a few key pools: Combat 7, Athletics 5, Subterfuge 4, etc. Then give them the normal seven health levels (or four for mooks). <br /> <br /> Sometimes I'll assign a fixed number to their best pool/area of specialism, less to a secondary area of expertise and the least to their tertiary or least favoured area of competence. So 8/6/4 or 5/3/2, etc.<br /> <br /> I sometimes also use the pregen character sheets from various adventures, and then use whichever one fits best in the circumstances, perhaps changing traits to better fit (so if there's a Tzimisce surgeon but I need a Toreador surgeon, I'll change Vicissitude and Animalism to Presence and Celerity, for example).<br /> <br /> Children of the Night and books like that can be very useful. You just change names and things, and use whichever characters most closely fit the concept you need.<br /> <br /> V5 suggests you do the Physical/Mental/Social thing with some key dice pools picked out, such as Athletics, Melee or whatever.<br /> <br /> I also use online character generators to print off a load of NPCs, and just throw those in when necessary.<br /> <br /> I try to keep it simple, so I use whatever is to hand at the time. If I've got nothing to hand, I just wing it, using the PCs' pools as a guide.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T01:27:37-07:00","id":1485177,"title":"","content":"I'm using V20 and W20 books, it get difficult to manage when some vampire npc use celerity while other not, so either I write down the pool I set or I use a complete character sheet for the moment, that's why I try to simplify things","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T02:39:29-07:00","id":1485180,"title":"","content":"Remember, the NPC's don't need to have a bloodpool, or any other energy/power stat. You know how much they can spend and you know how much they can do. Celerity can be a bit of a pain, but my recommendation would be to use less characters with celerity just to simplify things for yourself. If you want to make it even more straightforward as far as the number of rolls go, you could even allow One roll to apply to all the actions done with Celerity in that round.<br /> <br /> And you could also reserve Celerity as a special kind of opponent, one that's quite a bit more worrying, while any regular opposition you need to fight can just use other powers, or simple physical powers.<br /> <br /> Oh, and most NPC's won't even need that level of detail, as you'll just be interacting with them, not going to fight them.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T10:14:35-07:00","id":1485193,"title":"","content":"It's been a year since i began to masterize at the same time V20 and W20 and It's been reaaly difficult for me to find a way to manage my game, so thank Asmodai for the good trick.<br /> <br /> I'm still not out of troubles anyway, cause my player are a bit to imaginative, one of them play a tremere and asked if he could rip out or move the heart of a target with movement of the mind path and create ritual to put some effect of thaumaturgy and his discipline in his stuff (theft of vitae on a sword, Fortitude on his kevlar that kind of stuff)<br /> Those are interesting idea but it's a real pain to try and fix something for everything. I end up telling them just no, to not overcharge my work on the story","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T16:41:45-07:00","id":1485225,"title":"","content":"My NPCs have exactly the traits I need them to have. And what they dont need, they dont have.<br /> <br /> Only BBB (big-bad-bosses) gain a sheet, and only if relevant. If for example my BBB used &quot;summon ugly demon&quot; on the players, if I plan for my villain to show up again, then I'll mark &quot;ugly demon summoning&quot; on the sheet. Otherwise, my baddies remain in a quantum state of uncertanty.<br /> <br /> What's more, mooks usually have the base average pool. The guard with the gun, 6 dices to shoot. That's his only purpose on life, to have 6 dices to shoot players that fail stealth. Likewise, all the other mooks that need to shoot will likewise have 6 dices to shoot... What a coincidence right.<br /> <br /> If the guard needs to have Will at all, his willpower will be 3. ALL guards in the entire game world have will 3.<br /> <br /> Other traits dont exist until they are relevant, and the observers collapse the quantum wave revealing them. Until they do thou, those traits remain under the quantum uncertanty. How many dots do my mook have in occultism? Dont know, dont care. Until such a day comes where this becomes necessary, his rating of Occultism exists in an uncertain wave where is between 0 to 5, all at the same time. Until such a day comes where the observers (players) collapse the wave of probabilities and determine that it's actually 0 or 3 or 5.<br /> <br /> The only thing important to note is that, once you've determined one trait for a NPC, stick with it if said NPC is bound to return. If not it doesnt matter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-28T17:48:19-07:00","id":1485241,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485193#post1485193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's been a year since i began to masterize at the same time V20 and W20 and It's been reaaly difficult for me to find a way to manage my game, so thank Asmodai for the good trick.<br /> <br /> I'm still not out of troubles anyway, cause my player are a bit to imaginative, one of them play a tremere and asked if he could rip out or move the heart of a target with movement of the mind path and create ritual to put some effect of thaumaturgy and his discipline in his stuff (theft of vitae on a sword, Fortitude on his kevlar that kind of stuff)<br /> Those are interesting idea but it's a real pain to try and fix something for everything. I end up telling them just no, to not overcharge my work on the story</div> </div> </div> </div>Glad it was of use. A long time ago I started out similarly to you, and only after running a couple of sessions did I realise that you don't really need that level of granularity for the game.<br /> <br /> You are the final arbiter of how any power works, not the player, so if you don't like their interpretation don't allow it. Heck, if you don't like certain powers feel free to limit them, just be clear and consistant about it with your group.<br /> <br /> Since your question about bloodboil made me take out my V20... none of your players interpretations are how the game works - Movement of the Mind cannot do damage to people except by tossing objects at them at Level 4+, it's quite specific about that. Also it speaks of objects as discrete units, which means that an &quot;object&quot; is a human being, not a pieces of it. There are rituals to enchant items and weapons in Thaumaturgy, however they are not as effective as full disciplines (they give minor buffs mostly to what the item actually does) and are quite costly to make.<br /> <br /> I'd like to leave you with some words of wisdom coined in the darkest days of Exalted when players were trying to be horribly abusive with how they used the mechanics:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Chicanery-No: If a player uses this Charm in an abusive or exploitative manner, the ST may punch him right in the goddamn face.&quot;<br /> -TheDementedOne (Robert Vance) </div> </div> </div>(please don't take this literally, the idea behind this quote is that just because someone is being creative in an abusive way doesn't mean it's allowed or should be encouraged)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":1182,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","user":"Asmodai","date":". 06-28-2022, 08:50 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-29T16:05:08-07:00","id":1485318,"title":"","content":"Well, any important character in my games is going to have a full and detailed character sheet just as the players (most of the time, even more detailed than the players lol, as I keep track of what each of the most important characters are doing and their history, their interests, etc, while players most of the time tend to be more simplistic). Unless you mean &quot;nameless&quot; NPCs I think I didn't understand the question very well...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T02:44:41-07:00","id":1485346,"title":"","content":"Well i was talking about random anemy, of course the important characters have a full sheet, but i was looking for a way to manage random npc's, the anonym victim or guards","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T04:22:46-07:00","id":1485352,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485346#post1485346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well i was talking about random anemy, of course the important characters have a full sheet, but i was looking for a way to manage random npc's, the anonym victim or guards</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Oh, naturally! As already mentioned by other posts, in this case, it's best to have a very basic general dicepools for them. I think it makes things easier, just write their general dicepool and equipment, etc, it works fine. It's good to have some names' list at hand, just in case some PC gets interested in them. I had once a generic gangrel who was very unimportant to the story (he was basically just one underling of an important npc), but the PCs for a reason made him important, so I ended up giving him a complete character sheet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T04:24:28-07:00","id":1485353,"title":"","content":"But then again, most of the time, I avoid using &quot;nameless npcs&quot; (what as a consequence, unfortunately makes my planning much harder as I waste a good amount of time making character's sheets, I wish I were less &quot;perfectionist&quot;).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T05:59:51-07:00","id":1485359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485353#post1485353\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But then again, most of the time, I avoid using &amp;quot;nameless npcs&amp;quot; (what as a consequence, unfortunately makes my planning much harder as I waste a good amount of time making character's sheets, I wish I were less &amp;quot;perfectionist&amp;quot<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />.</div> </div> </div> </div>I understand that feeling, i can't stop myself thinking that every character are different so they should have various capacity... so various complete character sheet 'x)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T07:17:52-07:00","id":1485368,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485346#post1485346\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well i was talking about random anemy, of course the important characters have a full sheet, but i was looking for a way to manage random npc's, the anonym victim or guards</div> </div> </div> </div>Even those don't need it. In my campaign notes major supporting characters and antagonists have way more story details and relationships then mechanical notes. They will usually have a trademark skill or trick that I might note which might denote power, but they don't need a shit. What their themes, desires and relationships is way more relevant than fixed numbers, and once you note those down the numbers will come to you naturally.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T08:07:14-07:00","id":1485372,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485368#post1485368\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Even those don't need it. In my campaign notes major supporting characters and antagonists have way more story details and relationships then mechanical notes. They will usually have a trademark skill or trick that I might note which might denote power, but they don't need a shit. What their themes, desires and relationships is way more relevant than fixed numbers, and once you note those down the numbers will come to you naturally.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. Exactly this. Even the BBB (big bad bosses) dont need sheets, they only need the &quot;typical&quot; dice pools - which ultimately will define how &quot;hard&quot; they are and their &quot;modus operandi&quot; - ie their story and some relevant traits and background.<br /> <br /> You'll only define a trait for your NPCs if it's relevant - for example, my Big Evil likes to &quot;get physical&quot;, so he has celerity and potence 3-5 (that value will depend on how capable my players are and if I want that Big Evil to be unbeatable by them, beatable with difficult or a joke), with a fix &quot;physical pool&quot; between 4 to 10 (again, depending on how hard I want him to be - if I want my BBB to be too much to the players, they most come to their senses that fighting him would be stupid and look for another way). The rest doesnt matter, until such a time when in does, and when that matters I &quot;summon&quot; his traits out of thin air.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T08:27:35-07:00","id":1485378,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Even those don't need it. In my campaign notes major supporting characters and antagonists have way more story details and relationships then mechanical notes. They will usually have a trademark skill or trick that I might note which might denote power, but they don't need a shit. What their themes, desires and relationships is way more relevant than fixed numbers, and once you note those down the numbers will come to you naturally. </div> </div> </div>They should certainly have more &quot;themes, desires and relationships&quot;, but they also need mechanical notes, mine have both. I'm not very fond of &quot;playing pretend&quot;, there's a fine line between RPG and &quot;play pretend&quot;, but it's dangerous when one crosses this boundary...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T08:44:34-07:00","id":1485380,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485378#post1485378\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They should certainly have more &quot;themes, desires and relationships&quot;, but they also need mechanical notes, mine have both. I'm not very fond of &quot;playing pretend&quot;, there's a fine line between RPG and &quot;play pretend&quot;, but it's dangerous when one crosses this boundary...</div> </div> </div> </div>That's your opinion, and rather unfair to anyone else not using your methodology.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T08:55:17-07:00","id":1485383,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485380#post1485380\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's your opinion, and rather unfair to anyone else not using your methodology.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Certainly, it's just an opinion. I prefer to be ready in these cases, I already improvise a lot, so in somethings I like to be &quot;methodical&quot;, so to speak. In any case, to be honest, I have many times ST games completely &quot;on the fly&quot;, creating traits &quot;out of thin air&quot;, etc. But in the end, I realized that it depended too much on how I was, when you're feeling great and inspired it might work, but some other times it does not. As someone with Bipolar Disorder, I now prefer to have most things detailed before the game, so that I have a &quot;safe port&quot; in case I'm not feeling &quot;all that great&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T08:57:05-07:00","id":1485384,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485378#post1485378\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> They should certainly have more &amp;quot;themes, desires and relationships&amp;quot;, but they also need mechanical notes, mine have both. I'm not very fond of &amp;quot;playing pretend&amp;quot;, there's a fine line between RPG and &amp;quot;play pretend&amp;quot;, but it's dangerous when one crosses this boundary...</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, they have mechanical notes. Guards have will 2-3, physical pool 5-6, social pool NA (not relevant) and mental pool 3-5. That's all I need to know about my NPCs.<br /> <br /> As for the BBBs, those pools will simply be larger (to be a challenge to the PCs) and they'll have some &quot;signature traits&quot;. If my hot headed &quot;spank 'em!&quot; BBB will only ever solve his problems punching away, he'll have Potence-Celerity-Fortitude 3-5 (or more depending on the players). My Technocrat baddie will have his &quot;Big-Blaster-Gun&quot; and maybe his &quot;cheat pool of Luck&quot; (Entropy 2), but I dont need to listen his Time 1 Sphere or Life 1 or any other Spheres he'll only ever use on the lab. He has his tricks and the equip he bears with him, and that's enough. IF there's something that will be a trademark of his - for example his BBG (big blaster gun), I mark it on his notes, and THIS one he'll carry around in future battles (if this is a villain that has a future).<br /> <br /> Also, his dice pools being fixed means Im not &quot;cheatting&quot; my players - his pool is pre-determined just like the players sheets, it's just that Im not specifying how much Animal Empathy or Ettiquete my Big Baddy has because that will never be relevant for his role in the story, so instead I just determine a &quot;generic dice pool&quot; which is how competent that NPC will be AT THE THINGS THAT'LL MATTER.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T10:12:49-07:00","id":1485391,"title":"","content":"My advice is to set an amount of dice pools for each type of &quot;challenge&quot;, in physical, social and mental interactions, compared to the PCs.<br /> <br /> For example, if your players are neonates, any dice pool that is 3 above the average is a challenge to them - but please do take notice of their min-max, both between their own sheets and amoung the table (the typical combination of &quot;bricks&quot; and &quot;blasters&quot; and &quot;mentalists&quot; that optimize the players when combined). Sometimes this has to be reduced to only 2 above - for example, if you send a &quot;physical&quot; villain on their way, but they are a bunch of guys with basically only Auspex and there&quot;s just a single player who is a &quot;muscle&quot;, 2 dice above the physical average of the muscle of the table will already be a big challenge and a good enough BBB for them all.<br /> <br /> Big Bad Bosses however dont just come out in the open - not even the physical types. They'll ALWAYS use underlings, and the final confrontation against one is the result of a long string of events that thwarts his plans.<br /> <br /> I like to use BBBs NOT for a Final Confrontation - but rather AFTER THE END. After the players utterly ruined ALL the BBB plans, that's when he comes on the open - and now it's no longer part of his games, now it's personnal, the BBB wants REVENGE, he lost his resources and is now acting in person fueled by hate; the Big Evil has already lost, so now he'll hit the players where it hurts - a physical elder may come rushing in frenzy, but a mental or social one may turn on your dependants, allies, loved ones etc. This may or may not end up in a blaze of glory for him.<br /> Think of &quot;The scoure of the Shire&quot; by Saruman after the war of the ring was already over; the Big Bad cannot win, but he'll make sure the players pay in kind.<br /> <br /> The same goes for all the others, but outside of physical you can go a little deeper, except if you're considering breaking powers (such as Domination); usually confrontations against the BBB that arent physical wont involve death risk, so your BBB can come as high as 4 dots above the average social pools of your players (or 3 above the best &quot;face&quot; of the table), and that will usually mean he convinces third parties to turn on the players and such, and it's going to be a challenge to disarm his plans (as opposed to direct confrontation).<br /> <br /> As for the mental pool, it depends on either that's for combat or not; 4 above is a challenge if not for combat, 3 if it is. This means the BBB gets a better security software than the players can, or is aways 2 steps ahead of them, or the player has to have some insane blind luck to be able to get a jump on him using Obfuscate (which for the Big Boss should ALWAYS be a bad idea).<br /> <br /> As for Rivals, I define those as adversaries that are roughly at the same level as the players; usually, no single Rival is a challenge for the entire players' group, so they'll only ever make their move if they manage to isolate a single player (and I like to have Rivals that have a particular &quot;affinity&quot; to one of the players instead of all), but one thing that I like to do is the &quot;anti-party&quot;, a GROUP of NPCs roughly the same &quot;challenging rate&quot; as the PCs, and I usually pair those with each single player as their &quot;focus&quot;.<br /> <br /> For Rivals, you either have them have the same average pools the players have, or 1 or at most 2 above. One trick I like to do here however is to use what I call &quot;Reverse Rivals&quot;. It works like this: the Rival of a &quot;Brick&quot; player, instead of also being Physical, could be a social &quot;Face&quot; or a mental &quot;Genius&quot;. Therefore, for example, if the player has prioritized Physical/Mental/Social, the Rival could instead have Social/Mental/Physical, and would challenge the player in an entirely different way; you can even min max this, giving him 1 or 2 dices ABOVE the average Physical Pool of the player (in his Social Pool) while giving him at the same time 1 or 2 dices BELLOW the player's Social in his Physical. That would make a pathetically weak Rival, but a master manipulator to confront the big strong player.<br /> <br /> Bellow those are the Underlings, those should have 1 or 2 dice pools bellow the players. Now, it is entirely possible to have important Underlings of note however, those who are weaker than the players but trying to catch up with strong obstination. Such Underlings can still be a challenge, particularly for their annoying tendency to keep trying despite their failures (Hunters for example make a good example of challenging Underlings).<br /> <br /> Bellow those are the Mooks, those are the faceless crowds that exist only as Cannonfodder against the players or minor inconveniences (the typical Guard). Those will always be bellow in everything, at least 2 or 3 dices bellow the players in everything (except in exceptional circunstances), and very limited in powers and resources.<br /> <br /> BBBs can make use of Rivals, Underlings or Mooks mostly at will; Rivals will usually only use Underlings or Mooks, while Underlings will usually be the one &quot;mini bosses&quot; for groups of Mooks; however, Rivals and persistant Underlings can sometimes &quot;summon&quot; a BBB for assistance, if they are constantly taking a beat from the players.<br /> <br /> Finally, the last category are the &quot;Demi-Gods&quot;. Those are so above ALL the players that there's no challenge - therefore, no need for Stats AT ALL. The &quot;game mechanic&quot; here is simple: one of those shows up, you lose. Period. If they want something, you give to them; if not, they take, and there's nothing you can do about it.<br /> <br /> Now, Demi-Gods dont just show out of the blue. Also, NEVER &quot;summon&quot; one to simply destroy the players - for example &quot;I come here to replace the Prince, and I cant suffer his supporters to live, I need my next Pawn to be fully supported in this city; since you guys are supporters of the Prince, Im sorry but you gotta go&quot;. Dont do that. This kind of NPC serves better as a mystery - and he'll NEVER just outright burn the players alive with a funny eye. Even if the players ATTACK him, he will just effortessly subdue them, like an adult playing with children, and then make some condescending remarks like &quot;just stay out of my way children... I have no time to waste on you... And in future, try to be smarter, or you wont last long in this world...&quot;<br /> <br /> As for the funny stuff - the supernatural mojo - anything goes, just make sure to have those fit the concepts of your NPCs and to balance those up to the &quot;Challenge Level&quot; they represent (in relation to the players). If your players are neonates with a single dot in 3 disciplines, so too would a Rival &quot;Brick&quot; Brujah only have a single dot in Potence and Celerity - and if he's NEVER going to challenge the players socially, there's no reason to bother with Presence. If the BBB is a Lasombra, he obviously will have high Tenebrosity, but other than that just assign ANY powers that would fit him, even outside the clan - so high levels of Potence, Fortitude and Celerity if he's physical, Dominate and Presence if social, Auspex and Obtenebration if mental etc. And in order to not need to check all powers constantly, just mark a few &quot;signature moves&quot;, which is what he'll ALWAYS use - like Presence 5 for example (and ignore the rest if you never plan to use those).<br /> You can also give them new, cool and unheard of powers too, no need to stick to tables; that's easier to justify for lvl 6+ disciplines or ones such as thaumaturgy or necromancy, but dont feel too bound by that thou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T12:02:55-07:00","id":1485401,"title":"","content":"If I would storyteller this way, I would not even be motivated to be a Storyteller. Just reading oversimplification makes me want to not think about the game, even when I started, more than 20 years ago, I always gave unique traits to my characters, they're not there simply to &quot;be a challenge&quot; or to interact as a nameless video-game rpg NPC. But then again, I'm also a writer, writing about my characters makes me feel good...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T12:34:30-07:00","id":1485405,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485368#post1485368\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Even those don't need it. In my campaign notes major supporting characters and antagonists have way more story details and relationships then mechanical notes. They will usually have a trademark skill or trick that I might note which might denote power, but they don't need a shit. What their themes, desires and relationships is way more relevant than fixed numbers, and once you note those down the numbers will come to you naturally.</div> </div> </div> </div>At one point in time, I would have been horrified by this mentality, but it's one I try to adopt as much as possible now.<br /> <br /> The terrible thing for many STs to admit is that 90% of our preparation work is irrelevant to actually running the game. We provide more details in our game notes than is needed at the gaming table. Writing complete PC style character sheets for NPCs falls into that category.<br /> <br /> The reason most of us do that (and other superfluous things about the game) is because it's fun. (And because it's what see in sourcebooks for NPCs.) We like world building. We like creating characters and filling in dots on a character sheet. But at the end of the day, all we need to determine is &quot;What size dice pool should I be using for this character for this type of action?&quot; The Storyteller system actually makes this much easier than other game systems because its dice pool system makes a lot stats inherently generic.<br /> <br /> In terms of productive use of time, most STs only need to write up some very brief generic stats for major character types. Those will work at least 80% of the time. For that other 20%, you can go into more detail, at whatever level may be needed for what you want to do with them. STs shouldn't think that if they don't have completely statted NPCs that they are somehow &quot;cheating&quot;. It's not.<br /> <br /> I'm actually a GM who massively overprepares when I run a game. The reason I do is I don't do &quot;on the fly&quot; very well. Some GMs are great at it, and their games never suffer. Others do it, but fail miserably because their &quot;on the fly&quot; decisions don't make sense and undermine the campaign. In order for me to do things &quot;on the fly&quot; well, I need to do a lot of prep work beforehand by working out the most likely things that will come up during game myself. But that generally is about major things about the setting. &quot;PCs do stupid things, I need to figure out ahead of time what happens when they do the ones I've done myself or seen other players do at the table.&quot; &quot;This thing is bound to come up, so I need to be ready how the NPCs will handle it.&quot; And so on. It's not about determining individual stats for individual characters.<br /> <br /> It's not a problem if STs have fun creating NPC stats, and want to do that. Or if more detailed mechanics actually help them. But what I discovered over the years is that very often a GM can get away with a lot less prep work than they might expect especially when it comes to grindy things like statting out individual characters.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T13:17:58-07:00","id":1485411,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> they are somehow &quot;cheating&quot;. It's not. </div> </div> </div>It's not that, it's the fact that the game will NEVER be as good if you do everything &quot;on the fly&quot;, and trust me I've done it countless times, but it's never the same as with preparation, etc, no matter &quot;how good on the fly&quot; you are, simply because of a simple concept, &quot;energy management&quot;, if the ST is good at doing things &quot;on the fly&quot;, it's going to be IMMENSELY superior if he already has many things prepared, so that he can use his energy to work on ways to make those things even more interesting, etc.. And second, and perhaps more important is that it's UTTERLY UNFUN. I mean if I need to go to an event and ST just for kicks, I don't give a damn about that, I can do it on the fly, I've done it several times, but it's not the on the same level as one well-thought story, which gives meaning to every action and reaction and creates good immersion, etc, I use music in my games, I try to make everything as immersive as possible.<br /> I used to ST &quot;on the fly&quot; when I didn't care that much about RPG or writing, when I was more concerned with real life issues, etc. It's not the same, it's the worst thing any activity can be: It's BORING. It's like it's meaningless...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T13:19:49-07:00","id":1485412,"title":"","content":"I'm sorry, but I'm allergic to anything &quot;generic&quot; when it comes to writing, arts, etc.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T13:40:58-07:00","id":1485415,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485405#post1485405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> At one point in time, I would have been horrified by this mentality, but it's one I try to adopt as much as possible now.<br /> <br /> The terrible thing for many STs to admit is that 90% of our preparation work is irrelevant to actually running the game. We provide more details in our game notes than is needed at the gaming table. Writing complete PC style character sheets for NPCs falls into that category.<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> It's not a problem if STs have fun creating NPC stats, and want to do that. Or if more detailed mechanics actually help them. But what I discovered over the years is that very often a GM can get away with a lot less prep work than they might expect especially when it comes to grindy things like statting out individual characters.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that's what a couple of decades of hindsight does to you. I feel I was pretty lucky to figure &quot;quickly&quot; that you don't need attributes and abilities, just numbers for NPC's, but I really, really thought I had to follow the same principles for the NPC's as I did for the PC's. I'm kinda surprised it took White Wolf so long to actually introduce simplified stats for NPCS, the way the antagonists are written in the core is just too easy to lead you into thinking that everything has to run on the same elements.<br /> <br /> I've also moved more towards building the setting and try to make it robust enough to endure the PC's and allow me to tell stories or just wing things based on the weird things the players thought were cool and important. For that building characters and relationships while giving them thematic powers really helps making things tick and sessions fly.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-06-30T13:44:45-07:00","id":1485416,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485412#post1485412\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm sorry, but I'm allergic to anything &quot;generic&quot; when it comes to writing, arts, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>You are writing a story to tell with your friends, and you're editing it on the fly with every change they introduce due to how their brains and characters work differently than yours. There is a lot of creativity, imagination and original content created through this. Now if you focus on making this experience better instead of bean counting things that don't contribute to this story, that's your choice, but by focusing on overgranular mechanics you are investing energy and time into things that are not visible to the players instead of on what is.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T00:22:08-07:00","id":1485458,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485067#post1485067\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't make sheets for them. Assign them a pool for tasks they're good at (I.e. Physical 6, Mental 4, Social 3) and give them any power that seems suitable to them as a character. The fine nuances and mechanics are irrelevant. They are there to serve a narrative purpose, not to force you to have a million extra sheets to bean count.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I go one step even simpler, I rate (some) NPCs simply on Primary and Secondary Dice Pools, so it's as simple as 'Name 6/4'. Primary is for stuff they're good at, and Secondary is everything else. That just leaves things like choosing attacks/weapons and any special abilities.<br /> <br /> Obviously for NPCs that the players are likely to interact with a lot I flesh them out a bit better but doing it this way saves a lot of time. Here's a snippet from my current campaign:<br /> <br /> Mages<ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Augustus Koehne, Verbena Rune Mage (8/5)<br /> Member of the Iron Circle<br /> Arete 4<br /> Spheres: Forces 4, Matter 3, Entropy 3, Prime 2</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Oscar 'Leo' Schilling, Ghoul Sorcerer (8/5)<br /> Member of the Thule Gesellschaft<br /> Disciplines: Potence 3<br /> Mythic Numina: Shapeshifting 3</span></li> </ol>As you can see for me the important points are their name, association, and powers - the dice pools are simply '(8/5)' are to give a rough idea of their relative power level and frankly most of the time I'd list just one number if I wouldn't forget what it was for!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11490,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","user":"Dogstar","date":". 07-01-2022, 03:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T10:02:55-07:00","id":1485516,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485458#post1485458\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I go one step even simpler, I rate (some) NPCs simply on Primary and Secondary Dice Pools, so it's as simple as 'Name 6/4'. Primary is for stuff they're good at, and Secondary is everything else. That just leaves things like choosing attacks/weapons and any special abilities.<br /> <br /> Obviously for NPCs that the players are likely to interact with a lot I flesh them out a bit better but doing it this way saves a lot of time. Here's a snippet from my current campaign:<br /> <br /> Mages<ol class=\"decimal\"><li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Augustus Koehne, Verbena Rune Mage (8/5)<br /> Member of the Iron Circle<br /> Arete 4<br /> Spheres: Forces 4, Matter 3, Entropy 3, Prime 2</span></li> <li><span style=\"font-family:Calibri\">Oscar 'Leo' Schilling, Ghoul Sorcerer (8/5)<br /> Member of the Thule Gesellschaft<br /> Disciplines: Potence 3<br /> Mythic Numina: Shapeshifting 3</span></li> </ol>As you can see for me the important points are their name, association, and powers - the dice pools are simply '(8/5)' are to give a rough idea of their relative power level and frankly most of the time I'd list just one number if I wouldn't forget what it was for!</div> </div> </div> </div>Personnally i will set 3 values, 1 for each attributes and define the pool depending on which their better at, like physical 8 social 4 and mental 6<br /> You're right about precise their association, it's certainly usefull to easily recognise them when PC get interested in them","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T10:15:42-07:00","id":1485518,"title":"","content":"For the &quot;demigod&quot; npc's just saying to my pc they can't do anything isn't fair to me, they should be able to react, and if they succed by a wise plan or a spectacular success then the game will take a very satisfying turn even if the purpose will certainly be running away.<br /> <br /> Problem is that the game system isn't set for godly characters, a vampire of the 3th generation can get to 10 in their stats, they could have Dex 10 + Brawl 10 + Celerity 10, once together around the table, roll 30 dice bits by bits will just slowing down the game<br /> It's been a while since i began to think about a system for those characters, that's hard but i'm sure It's possible","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T13:20:42-07:00","id":1485539,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485518#post1485518\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For the &quot;demigod&quot; npc's just saying to my pc they can't do anything isn't fair to me, they should be able to react, and if they succed by a wise plan or a spectacular success then the game will take a very satisfying turn even if the purpose will certainly be running away.<br /> <br /> Problem is that the game system isn't set for godly characters, a vampire of the 3th generation can get to 10 in their stats, they could have Dex 10 + Brawl 10 + Celerity 10, once together around the table, roll 30 dice bits by bits will just slowing down the game<br /> It's been a while since i began to think about a system for those characters, that's hard but i'm sure It's possible</div> </div> </div> </div>That's why you don't fight them directly. You fight them indirectly, you seek items to incapcitate them or drive them away or siphon their power, allies that can even the odds and generally change the odds in your favour. There is zero point in stating a thing that's just a walking talking narrative device. You will play around it anyway, because it just kills the regular PC group. So you make it into a story to take them out through means other than direct conflict. That's why neither the Antedelluvians nor the Wyrm need stats all that much.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T13:43:39-07:00","id":1485543,"title":"","content":"Of course, it's interesting to just find a way to weaken them. The fact is in my Vtm campaign my players choosed the &quot;darkside&quot; and are hunting lower generation vampire to steal their powers. They want to replace the clan founders x)<br /> <br /> It's a cool campaign and i just make them run after ghost, meeting new situation and people, somekind of eternal treasure hunting<br /> In my werewolf campaign they're trying to find the other ferae and create an alliance between all tribes and race to face the apocalypse and organise a last battle in malfea at the end of the world<br /> Ambitious in both case that's why i needed some tips to manage my npc's","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26839,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","user":"Cain Loup-Noir","date":". 07-01-2022, 05:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T15:06:10-07:00","id":1485548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485401#post1485401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If I would storyteller this way, I would not even be motivated to be a Storyteller. Just reading oversimplification makes me want to not think about the game, even when I started, more than 20 years ago, I always gave unique traits to my characters, they're not there simply to &amp;quot;be a challenge&amp;quot; or to interact as a nameless video-game rpg NPC. But then again, I'm also a writer, writing about my characters makes me feel good...</div> </div> </div> </div>In the end of the day, the super awesome and super interesting, complex and absolutely extraordinary villain that you spent weeks concocting may be done in a literal stroke of a dice. <br /> <br /> In other times, it can happen that the insignificant mook which you just named &quot;Bob&quot; so that it could even have a name for that moment and that wasnt supposed to be anything more than part of the furniture, grows out to become truly memorable (and yes, this has happened to me).<br /> <br /> The ST has to understand that this is not like writting, we are indeed creating a story, but that aint a solo creation. Your best plans will come to dust in face of player creativity - and you know what, I love it when that happens, I love it when my players surprise me. Which is why I tend to be VERY lenient to them - you'll never see me trying to restrict my players because they managed to explore a loophole I hadnt thought about before.<br /> <br /> Therefore I dont set my plans in stone - ie I dont create a story that is supposed to have a middle and an end, and rather what I usually do is create worlds that feel alive, and THAT&quot;S what I invest a lot of energy into, which is why Im always trying to simulate the biggest amount of realism to the scenery in my head; so for example, I do the math of the expected vampiric population, the hierarquical positions, the connections, the important people relevant to the setting (if a town, the Xeriff, the Mayor, major companies, jornalists etc) and yes some NPCs will be a little generic, but tell me, in a movie how many backgrounds of the characters in play to we get? In horror movies we get a history of the main players, but the exorcist priest is always a &quot;generic priest&quot;. The cop is always generic too.<br /> <br /> You get a bit more info on the main stars/villains, but that's it. We dont need info on the clerk of the hotel - unless the story being told is about him.<br /> <br /> And you know what? This creates AMAZING stories.<br /> <br /> Game of Thrones was like that (when it ressembled anything marginally good.).<br /> <br /> The problem of game of Thrones is that it is a book - and making open ended stories for books/series is absolutely terrible, because those are supposed to end. You end up with the shit show that was GoT or Lost.<br /> <br /> RPG however is not supposed to end - you are after all telling the daily life of someone, and that only ends with death. In the movies it ends with &quot;and they lived happily ever after&quot;, but on RPGs it doesnt end there.<br /> <br /> You should give it a try one day, you would surprise yourself with how good the results can be and how many awesome stories simply naturally come to existance out of the blue this way","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T15:40:20-07:00","id":1485552,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485405#post1485405\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> At one point in time, I would have been horrified by this mentality, but it's one I try to adopt as much as possible now.<br /> <br /> The terrible thing for many STs to admit is that 90% of our preparation work is irrelevant to actually running the game. We provide more details in our game notes than is needed at the gaming table. Writing complete PC style character sheets for NPCs falls into that category.<br /> <br /> The reason most of us do that (and other superfluous things about the game) is because it's fun. (And because it's what see in sourcebooks for NPCs.) We like world building. We like creating characters and filling in dots on a character sheet. But at the end of the day, all we need to determine is &amp;quot;What size dice pool should I be using for this character for this type of action?&amp;quot; The Storyteller system actually makes this much easier than other game systems because its dice pool system makes a lot stats inherently generic.<br /> <br /> In terms of productive use of time, most STs only need to write up some very brief generic stats for major character types. Those will work at least 80% of the time. For that other 20%, you can go into more detail, at whatever level may be needed for what you want to do with them. STs shouldn't think that if they don't have completely statted NPCs that they are somehow &amp;quot;cheating&amp;quot;. It's not.<br /> <br /> I'm actually a GM who massively overprepares when I run a game. The reason I do is I don't do &amp;quot;on the fly&amp;quot; very well. Some GMs are great at it, and their games never suffer. Others do it, but fail miserably because their &amp;quot;on the fly&amp;quot; decisions don't make sense and undermine the campaign. In order for me to do things &amp;quot;on the fly&amp;quot; well, I need to do a lot of prep work beforehand by working out the most likely things that will come up during game myself. But that generally is about major things about the setting. &amp;quot;PCs do stupid things, I need to figure out ahead of time what happens when they do the ones I've done myself or seen other players do at the table.&amp;quot; &amp;quot;This thing is bound to come up, so I need to be ready how the NPCs will handle it.&amp;quot; And so on. It's not about determining individual stats for individual characters.<br /> <br /> It's not a problem if STs have fun creating NPC stats, and want to do that. Or if more detailed mechanics actually help them. But what I discovered over the years is that very often a GM can get away with a lot less prep work than they might expect especially when it comes to grindy things like statting out individual characters.</div> </div> </div> </div>I spend a lot of time trying to build worlds that feel alive. I play WoD but I always convert all mechanics to Gurps, so for example, I did use a small python program to calculate for me the percentage distribution of each attributes among the population, just so I could have a ratio. Im creating an entire mini game inside the game of &quot;Realm Management&quot; for my fantasy games, that breeds life to nation-states, countries and Realms, taking into consideration any number of factors that you would like to factor in - production of sugar, tobbaco, coffee, wheat, cattle, water needs, fuel (charcoal, oil, wood, magic alchemical fuel), workforce by type, loyalty and conformness of the population, infraestructure, anyway, anything that you can imagine, levels of corruption and effectiveness of the bureocrats, administrators and higher ups, and turning all of that into a system that you role month to month, executing &quot;maneuvers&quot; to extract resources, rally armies and negogiate with neighbots. Now my next step is to turn this into an app or sheet to make all math automatic for me, just giving end results (after dice rolls). I want to use this system even without player imput, so my fantasy settings for example would have &quot;oh, a plague sets in into the realm&quot;, &quot;the realm is experiencing a drough&quot; - or when they go to the steriotypical &quot;quest giver tavern&quot; they may hear &quot;did you hear? It's been said that a vein of gold has been found in the dragons valley... People have gone nuts, selling all they have to try their luck there&quot;. So, rather than having the background being just background, I have it be alive, I have it affect the lives of everybody (including the players) and it gives a sense that somethings are out of their control; sometimes the random events can even bring hooks for advemtures! (the plague is a common one for fantasy for example).<br /> <br /> So, I spend most of the time thinking on history and geography, rather on who were my Bad Guy's school teachers. I think on what would happen to the local economy if there were a gold mine found in the edge of the city, or oil fund underground; why things in a given community are the way they are; instead of thinking about the entire 353 years of the city's prince (un)life story, I try to think what sort of vampiric Prince would arise from the city X. What sort of vampiric community or mage or were or leprechau community would blossom in this place. Then I think about history. Are the guys here the same that came with the city's first founders? What sorts of troubles did this city went through in the past? Plagues, war, famine? Has the population being dislodged?<br /> <br /> Those questions could, for example, mean a Prince that has (un)lived his entire existance in the same place, and thus he has a very firm and stablished control, with a very solid hierarchy for the local vampires and very few power grabs. This also means that while vampires may gossip and try to climb over rivals, the local population is much more traditional and everybody &quot;knows their place&quot;, if it's a city that hasnt been shaken all too much. A place like Warsow in Poland however, who has been devasted so many times over the centuries that is almost impossible to tell, would have a totally different culture, perhaps with a bunch of Anarchists and very unstable, with a weak prince and weak elders constantly trying to backstab each other.<br /> <br /> Once you start building the world, the personalities of said world present themselves to you without needing to create them","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T15:48:45-07:00","id":1485555,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485518#post1485518\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For the &amp;quot;demigod&amp;quot; npc's just saying to my pc they can't do anything isn't fair to me, they should be able to react, and if they succed by a wise plan or a spectacular success then the game will take a very satisfying turn even if the purpose will certainly be running away.<br /> <br /> Problem is that the game system isn't set for godly characters, a vampire of the 3th generation can get to 10 in their stats, they could have Dex 10 + Brawl 10 + Celerity 10, once together around the table, roll 30 dice bits by bits will just slowing down the game<br /> It's been a while since i began to think about a system for those characters, that's hard but i'm sure It's possible</div> </div> </div> </div>I was being more &quot;metaphysical&quot; than literal. Once upon a time, playing D&amp;D, I stopped an entire Orc invasion of thousands of Orcs against a few hundreds of resistance fighters - thanks to my Grease spell.<br /> <br /> Detail, I was a lvl 1 tribal human mage.<br /> <br /> Later on, in the same game, the orcs actually had dragons as allies.<br /> <br /> As it turned out, I suspect we werent meant to fight, but rather to escape.<br /> <br /> Well, I did managed to blind one of the dragons thou, with a natural 20 on the dice (which was the only way to do it). The blinded dragon hit a tower, and the soldiers killed it with ballistas. Absolutely epic.<br /> <br /> The thing thou, is that was an extreme stroke of luck. I knew I had to roll a natural 20, but I said &quot;what the hell, I'll try&quot;.<br /> <br /> Yes, your players can try. Maybe they can get luck. Most of the times they wont. My advice is not to kill them when they are in the way of a 4th generation simply because they failed to achieve 7 successes in a 5 pool dice.<br /> <br /> If they DO manage to beat up the &quot;unbeatables&quot;, kuddos to them, that'll be the topic of every RPG talk ever on your table, so yeah, I hope that can actually happens","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T15:59:51-07:00","id":1485557,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kakost</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485552#post1485552\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I spend a lot of time trying to build worlds that feel alive. I play WoD but I always convert all mechanics to Gurps, so for example, I did use a small python program to calculate for me the percentage distribution of each attributes among the population, just so I could have a ratio. Im creating an entire mini game inside the game of &quot;Realm Management&quot; for my fantasy games, that breeds life to nation-states, countries and Realms, taking into consideration any number of factors that you would like to factor in - production of sugar, tobbaco, coffee, wheat, cattle, water needs, fuel (charcoal, oil, wood, magic alchemical fuel), workforce by type, loyalty and conformness of the population, infraestructure, anyway, anything that you can imagine, levels of corruption and effectiveness of the bureocrats, administrators and higher ups, and turning all of that into a system that you role month to month, executing &quot;maneuvers&quot; to extract resources, rally armies and negogiate with neighbots. Now my next step is to turn this into an app or sheet to make all math automatic for me, just giving end results (after dice rolls). I want to use this system even without player imput, so my fantasy settings for example would have &quot;oh, a plague sets in into the realm&quot;, &quot;the realm is experiencing a drough&quot; - or when they go to the steriotypical &quot;quest giver tavern&quot; they may hear &quot;did you hear? It's been said that a vein of gold has been found in the dragons valley... People have gone nuts, selling all they have to try their luck there&quot;. So, rather than having the background being just background, I have it be alive, I have it affect the lives of everybody (including the players) and it gives a sense that somethings are out of their control; sometimes the random events can even bring hooks for advemtures! (the plague is a common one for fantasy for example).<br /> <br /> So, I spend most of the time thinking on history and geography, rather on who were my Bad Guy's school teachers. I think on what would happen to the local economy if there were a gold mine found in the edge of the city, or oil fund underground; why things in a given community are the way they are; instead of thinking about the entire 353 years of the city's prince (un)life story, I try to think what sort of vampiric Prince would arise from the city X. What sort of vampiric community or mage or were or leprechau community would blossom in this place. Then I think about history. Are the guys here the same that came with the city's first founders? What sorts of troubles did this city went through in the past? Plagues, war, famine? Has the population being dislodged?<br /> <br /> Those questions could, for example, mean a Prince that has (un)lived his entire existance in the same place, and thus he has a very firm and stablished control, with a very solid hierarchy for the local vampires and very few power grabs. This also means that while vampires may gossip and try to climb over rivals, the local population is much more traditional and everybody &quot;knows their place&quot;, if it's a city that hasnt been shaken all too much. A place like Warsow in Poland however, who has been devasted so many times over the centuries that is almost impossible to tell, would have a totally different culture, perhaps with a bunch of Anarchists and very unstable, with a weak prince and weak elders constantly trying to backstab each other.<br /> <br /> Once you start building the world, the personalities of said world present themselves to you without needing to create them</div> </div> </div> </div>Wow. I know there were bound to be some but I don't think I've encountered a simulationist like this running WoD before. Cool.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T16:01:08-07:00","id":1485558,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Cain Loup-Noir</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485543#post1485543\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, it's interesting to just find a way to weaken them. The fact is in my Vtm campaign my players choosed the &quot;darkside&quot; and are hunting lower generation vampire to steal their powers. They want to replace the clan founders x)<br /> <br /> It's a cool campaign and i just make them run after ghost, meeting new situation and people, somekind of eternal treasure hunting<br /> In my werewolf campaign they're trying to find the other ferae and create an alliance between all tribes and race to face the apocalypse and organise a last battle in malfea at the end of the world<br /> Ambitious in both case that's why i needed some tips to manage my npc's</div> </div> </div> </div>Have you considered Exalted, that might really be your group's speed?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T16:28:18-07:00","id":1485564,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485539#post1485539\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's why you don't fight them directly. You fight them indirectly, you seek items to incapcitate them or drive them away or siphon their power, allies that can even the odds and generally change the odds in your favour. There is zero point in stating a thing that's just a walking talking narrative device. You will play around it anyway, because it just kills the regular PC group. So you make it into a story to take them out through means other than direct conflict. That's why neither the Antedelluvians nor the Wyrm need stats all that much.</div> </div> </div> </div>This. Exactly this. Althought a level 1 Mage can make brilliant use of Grease to stop an orc invasion trying to cross over the only bridge access to town and have the insane luck of blinding a dragon, those should be the epic exceptions, not the norm. The thing is, dont throw a dragon to confront a lvl 1 party (and granted, the dragon was flying over our heads heading to the capital, completely ignoring us, so the GM didnt had us face one), but if you do, it's because the dragon is there for some other reason other than turning the players into dust. It either wants to talk for some reason, or has come to deliver a &quot;formal declaration of war&quot; from the &quot;dragon kingdom&quot; or some such.<br /> <br /> The only time you as the ST is fully within your rights to summon your entire bag of dices to apply as damage in the charaters, is when they decide on their own free will to step into the dragon's cave without some sort of plan or without making sure the dragon wont be there - ie sheer player stupidity. <br /> <br /> Other than that, the great Hriml\u00efch, the evil Lich Emperor, wont just casually climb down of his mountain just to pulverize the players out of the blue; if the evil lich DOES decide to come take a walk in the city, his victims will NOT be the players, but some other bunches of unluck annonimous faces in the crowd.<br /> <br /> And when the players do indeed seek to directly oppose the Cthulhus and the alien-monstruosities-gods of outer space or Psi-Galactic-Emperors and lich emperors of the world, they do it on their own terms - just like you described it, by eroding the Evil-King's power base and indirect confrontation, using cunning instead of brawl","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-01T17:25:30-07:00","id":1485576,"title":"","content":"Unfortunately no i haven't checked Exalted, why? Is there a special system for overpowered characters in it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-02T10:46:25-07:00","id":1485635,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kakost</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485552#post1485552\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I spend a lot of time trying to build worlds that feel alive. I play WoD but I always convert all mechanics to Gurps, so for example, I did use a small python program to calculate for me the percentage distribution of each attributes among the population, just so I could have a ratio. Im creating an entire mini game inside the game of &quot;Realm Management&quot; for my fantasy games, that breeds life to nation-states, countries and Realms, taking into consideration any number of factors that you would like to factor in - production of sugar, tobbaco, coffee, wheat, cattle, water needs, fuel (charcoal, oil, wood, magic alchemical fuel), workforce by type, loyalty and conformness of the population, infraestructure, anyway, anything that you can imagine, levels of corruption and effectiveness of the bureocrats, administrators and higher ups, and turning all of that into a system that you role month to month, executing &quot;maneuvers&quot; to extract resources, rally armies and negogiate with neighbots. Now my next step is to turn this into an app or sheet to make all math automatic for me, just giving end results (after dice rolls). I want to use this system even without player imput, so my fantasy settings for example would have &quot;oh, a plague sets in into the realm&quot;, &quot;the realm is experiencing a drough&quot; - or when they go to the steriotypical &quot;quest giver tavern&quot; they may hear &quot;did you hear? It's been said that a vein of gold has been found in the dragons valley... People have gone nuts, selling all they have to try their luck there&quot;. So, rather than having the background being just background, I have it be alive, I have it affect the lives of everybody (including the players) and it gives a sense that somethings are out of their control; sometimes the random events can even bring hooks for advemtures! (the plague is a common one for fantasy for example).<br /> <br /> So, I spend most of the time thinking on history and geography, rather on who were my Bad Guy's school teachers. I think on what would happen to the local economy if there were a gold mine found in the edge of the city, or oil fund underground; why things in a given community are the way they are; instead of thinking about the entire 353 years of the city's prince (un)life story, I try to think what sort of vampiric Prince would arise from the city X. What sort of vampiric community or mage or were or leprechau community would blossom in this place. Then I think about history. Are the guys here the same that came with the city's first founders? What sorts of troubles did this city went through in the past? Plagues, war, famine? Has the population being dislodged?<br /> <br /> Those questions could, for example, mean a Prince that has (un)lived his entire existance in the same place, and thus he has a very firm and stablished control, with a very solid hierarchy for the local vampires and very few power grabs. This also means that while vampires may gossip and try to climb over rivals, the local population is much more traditional and everybody &quot;knows their place&quot;, if it's a city that hasnt been shaken all too much. A place like Warsow in Poland however, who has been devasted so many times over the centuries that is almost impossible to tell, would have a totally different culture, perhaps with a bunch of Anarchists and very unstable, with a weak prince and weak elders constantly trying to backstab each other.<br /> <br /> Once you start building the world, the personalities of said world present themselves to you without needing to create them</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I know that feel. I love worldbuilding and I always want to make my world feel &quot;alive&quot;. As I believe most good stories tend to be character-driven, I like to spend a good deal of time writing about my characters. As I usually tell my players, if they don't want to advance the plot, an NPC is most likely going to. So I like to keep track of what every important NPC is doing and interested in, etc. Writing about the characters always creates many hooks as well. &quot;In the end of the day&quot;, even if I'm with a group of players that don't do much to the story, at the very least I end up with good material to write about. That's my style, it's the way I have more fun. I also love to be surprised (in a good way) by player's solutions, I also don't like restricting them.<br /> <br /> Regarding &quot;trying it one day&quot;, as I said, when I started I was very young and I tended to ST more &quot;on the fly&quot;, but as I mentioned, with time I noticed that even if I could improvise well, the better prepared I was, would most of the time make the rest more interesting. So then I started to spend more time writing details, etc. I now prefer this way, but as mentioned, if I need to make everything on the fly I would do it. I also love when a &quot;nobody&quot; NPC becomes important because of player's actions, this happens more or less often. But, just as good as that is when you create an interesting character that the players get interested in, and they want to interact more with her, and as they interact more with her, you disclose more and more about the said character, it usually feels more &quot;natural&quot;, when you have prepared something about the character.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-02T13:31:15-07:00","id":1485643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485635#post1485635\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I believe most good stories tend to be character-driven</div> </div> </div> </div>That in my personal experience and personal opinion, is the case for RPGs, because that's an &quot;open experience&quot; type of story; for book, TV series etc, I believe the reverse to be true. If we take all the good stories - Lord of the Rings, Avatar (the Legend of Aang, not that crap remake of Pocahontas in Spaaaaaace), Dune (ie Star Wars for adults), Star Wars (excluding the corporate fanfic garbage from Disney) - they are all story driven.<br /> <br /> The greatest example of character driven stories for books and TV are Lost and Game of Thrones - both of which started great, but as expected, ended in an unspeakable shitshow of the bad, the worse and the ugly.<br /> <br /> When you're telling a story with beggining, middle and end, you need the damn begin, middle and end. Otherwise, if you just let things &quot;flow&quot;, when it's time to end the story you have no idea on how to do it, because your story never had a planned end to begin with, and even worse, you simply let it all drift so far all that it reaches an unsalvageable point.<br /> <br /> That's why for example I told from Season 1 of GoT that the show would never have an ending (a proper one), and that G.R.R.M, the author, will NEVER finish the books. Seriously, he has no intention of EVER finishing the books - because he knows that there's no possible satisfactory end to it, since he never thought about one. You can bet that he plans on dying without ever doing it.<br /> <br /> For RPGs however it's different. I mean, it would be good if you had planned beforehand at least how the Big Baddie Macguffin device of death can be disabled/destroyed. If you didnt, that's ok, because the players CAN flee for their lives and try their luck on a &quot;safer neighborhood&quot;, and thus begin a new arc even thou having failed the previous one.<br /> <br /> So yes, I'd say that for RPGs char driven stories work like a charm - but I wouldnt use that method for writting a book","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-02T14:10:23-07:00","id":1485648,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So yes, I'd say that for RPGs char driven stories work like a charm - but I wouldnt use that method for writting a book </div> </div> </div>Well, I'd say the first work that comes to my mind when talking about character-driven's story is Shakespeare's Hamlet, as well as Oscar's Wilde's The Portrait of Dorian Gray or even Anne Rice books in general. In any case, I'm not discussing which one works best for what, as in my opinion, whether the story is good or not depends on many factors and I didn't intend to start a discussion about which one is best or anything similar, etc, in other words I didn't mean to start a discussion on aesthetics. I just think that, even if a story would not be classified as &quot;charater-driven&quot;, what is in itself a long discussion, to have good characters almost guarantee that the story is going to be good. Like my friends who act often say, a really good actor can save almost any play. That's the main point of why I focus so much on character's creation and development.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12348-herr-meister","name":"Herr Meister","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12348_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1114"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-07-02T22:04:34-07:00","id":1485681,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Herr Meister</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1485050-how-do-you-manage-your-npc?p=1485648#post1485648\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, I'd say the first work that comes to my mind when talking about character-driven's story is Shakespeare's Hamlet, as well as Oscar's Wilde's The Portrait of Dorian Gray or even Anne Rice books in general. In any case, I'm not discussing which one works best for what, as in my opinion, whether the story is good or not depends on many factors and I didn't intend to start a discussion about which one is best or anything similar, etc, in other words I didn't mean to start a discussion on aesthetics. I just think that, even if a story would not be classified as &amp;quot;charater-driven&amp;quot;, what is in itself a long discussion, to have good characters almost guarantee that the story is going to be good. Like my friends who act often say, a really good actor can save almost any play. That's the main point of why I focus so much on character's creation and development.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, absolutely. Lord of the Rings for example could be said to be story ridden, and yet it has memorable characters, and so does to the Legend of Aang, with Zuko being one of the best made redemption arcs for a villain I have ever seem, or Les Miserables from Victor Hugo, which contains my favorite charater of all times, inspector Javert, the anti-hero villain that ends up commiting suicide once he realized he was wrong in his zealotry to hunt the former criminal Jean Valjean. Good characters will transform any story (and I dont know if Hamlet could be called char driven... But for Anne Rice, yes, I really hadnt thought about her, how silly of me).<br /> <br /> I dont, each person has their way. When I create the world, the characters simply &quot;spring to life&quot; for me, instead of the other way around, so that's my method. Nothing wrong with yours either, if that how best works for you and you have fun with it, that's great","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24689-kakost","name":"Kakost","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"586"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}